===3.0 L Nano {{Anchor|3.0|3.0 l|3.0 L|3.0l|3.0L}}===
A 3.0L [[V6]] twin-turbocharged gasoline direct-injection engine, derived from the 2.7 L EcoBoost, was released in 2016 that produces between 325 and 418 horsepower. The 3.0 L replaced the 3.7 L Ti-VCT [[Ford Cyclone engine#3.7 L|Cyclone]] V6 engine in various vehicles, including the [[Lincoln MKZ|MKZ]], [[Lincoln Continental|Continental]], [[Lincoln Aviator|Aviator]], [[Ford Explorer]] and is also found in the 2022 [[Ford Bronco]] Raptor. The 3.0-liter version of the engine was created by increasing the 2.7-liter's cylinder bore in the CGI-block from 83.0 millimeters to 85.3, and by lengthening piston stroke by 3.0 millimeters (to 86.0).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.chastangford.com/static/dealer-18817/pdf/Specs-Houston-TX.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200703082818/https://www.chastangford.com/static/dealer-18817/pdf/Specs-Houston-TX.pdf |archive-date=July 3, 2020 |title=2020 Ford Explorer Technical Specifications}}</ref> Unlike its 2.7L counterpart, it does not use a wet-belt driven oil pump, instead using the timing chain.{{Citation needed|date=July 2026}} The 3.0 L is currently only equipped with direct injection while the 2.7 L and 3.5 L engines feature port fuel and direct injection fuel systems.

